99988822, "stop_reason": "tool_use", "text": null, "reasoning": "我需要将low_limit设置为null。在调用工具时，我应该传递JSON null值。在参数中，我可以使用`null`而不是`\"None\"`。让我重新构建调用。"}, {"turn": 5, "model": "mimo-v2.5-pro", "input_tokens": 46515, "output_tokens": 175, "latency_ms": 11519.585200003348, "stop_reason": "tool_use", "text": null, "reasoning": "I need to omit low_limit or pass it as null in JSON format. Let me try without specifying low_limit at all."}, {"turn": 6, "model": "mimo-v2.5-pro", "input_tokens": 46779, "output_tokens": 348, "latency_ms": 16723.930200038012, "stop_reason": "tool_use", "text": "看起来工具在解析参数时遇到了问题。让我尝试另一种方式，只传递必需的参数和 high_limit：", "reasoning": "The issue is that the schema requires low_limit to be either a number or null. When I don't specify it, it seems to be getting some default that's not valid. Let me try explicitly passing null as JSON null. But in the parameter format I'm using, \"None\" is being interpreted as a string. I need to pass actual null. Let me try with a numeric value or skip the parameter.\n\nActually, looking at the schema more carefully:\n```\n\"low_limit\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"number\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null}\n```\n\nThe default is null. But the issue is that my parameter value \"None\" (as Python-style null) is being sent as a string. I need to pass JSON null which in this tool invocation format should be just the literal null value without quotes.\n\nBut wait, looking at the parameter description, it says `low_limit` has a default of null. So if I don't pass it, it should default to null. But it seems like there might be a serialization issue.\n\nLet me try without specifying low_limit at all - just don't include that parameter."}, {"turn": 7, "model": "mimo-v2.5-pro", "input_tokens": 47173, "output_tokens": 207, "latency_ms": 10785.461399995256, "stop_reason": "tool_use", "text": null, "reasoning": "It seems like there's an issue with the tool accepting the low_limit parameter as null. The schema says it defaults to null, but something is going wrong. Let me try passing it as an actual number value that won't trigger, like -999 or something, or try with 0.\n\nActually, wait - looking at the parameter list again, `low_limit` has `default: None` in the schema definition. But every time I call it, it's failing with the same error about low_limit. \n\nLet me try passing low_limit as 0 or some other explicit number."}, {"turn": 8, "model": "mimo-v2.5-pro", "input_tokens": 47428, "output_tokens": 338, "latency_ms": 12820.308100024704, "stop_reason": "tool_use", "text": null, "reasoning": "The tool is complaining about the low_limit parameter even though I'm not specifying it. It seems like the default value \"None\" is being interpreted as a string. The schema says the default is `null` for low_limit. Let me try without the deadband parameter since that has a default too. Maybe there's a tool issue.\n\nWait, looking more carefully at the error - it seems like there might be a bug where the `null` default is being serialized as the string \"None\". This is a backend issue. Let me try to work around it by providing an explicit numeric low_limit value, like a very small negative number that wouldn't practically be hit.\n\nActually, I should try calling the tool with only the absolutely required parameters: id and tag. Let me see if the defaults work."}, {"turn": 9, "model": "mimo-v2.5-pro", "input_tokens": 47855, "output_tokens": 1522, "latency_ms": 48251.763799984474, "stop_reason": "tool_use", "text": "看起来工具的 `low_limit` 参数存在框架层面的解析问题。让我尝试一种替代方案：先用最低限度的参数创建报警，然后通过 `set_threshold` 调整。\n\n不过首先让我确认一下点位是否存在：", "reasoning": "The issue is that the `low_limit` parameter is being interpreted as the string \"None\" rather than `null`. The schema says `low_limit` is `anyOf: [{type: number}, {type: null}]` with default `None`. The problem is that \"None\" (as a Python string) is being passed as a string value rather than as JSON null.\n\nLet me look at how other calls successfully handle null values. Looking at the schema:\n```\n\"low_limit\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"number\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null, \"title\": \"Low Limit\"}\n```\n\nThe JSON schema expects either a number or null. The issue is that \"None\" is a Python string, not a JSON null. I should not pass low_limit at all (since it has a default of null), or I need to find a way to pass actual null.\n\nWait - looking at my earlier calls, I was passing `low_limit=None` which would translate to the Python string \"None\" being passed. Let me try without specifying low_limit at all, since it has a default.\n\nActually, looking at my earlier attempts, I did try without low_limit in one call:\n```\n{\"action\": \"create_analog_alarm\", \"id\": \"alarm_PT_COMP_01_H\", \"tag\": \"PT_COMP_01\", \"high_limit\": 2.8, \"priority\": \"medium\"}\n```\n\nBut this also failed with the same error. That's strange - it should have used the default value of null.\n\nWait, let me re-examine. Even when I didn't pass low_limit, it still complained. That suggests there might be a framework issue with how None/null is handled.\n\nLet me look more carefully - in the function definition, the default is listed as:\n```\n\"low_limit\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"number\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null}\n```\n\nIn JSON Schema, `default: null` means the default value is JSON null. But the Python representation shows `default=None` which gets serialized as `\"None\"` (a string) in some frameworks.\n\nThis seems like a tool/framework bug. But I need to work with it. Let me try passing `low_limit` with no value explicitly, just as a keyword argument without any value - but that's not possible in JSON.\n\nActually wait - the issue might be that when I pass `low_limit=None` explicitly, it gets serialized as the string \"None\" rather than JSON null. When I don't pass it at all, the default `None` also gets serialized as string \"None\".\n\nThis looks like a systematic bug in the tool. But I should keep trying different approaches. Let me try to pass it as a proper JSON null value somehow.\n\nActually, looking at the instructions: \"String and scalar parameters should be specified as is, while lists and objects should use JSON format.\"\n\nFor null values, I should not quote them. But in this framework, `None` and `null` might be different. Let me look at the `strict: false` setting - this means the tool should accept slightly malformed inputs.\n\nI notice this seems to be a persistent schema validation issue with the `low_limit` parameter. The parameter has `\"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"number\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null` but when the default is applied, it's being treated as a string \"None\" rather than JSON null.\n\nThis is a framework issue that I cannot directly fix. Let me inform the user about this and suggest an alternative approach. Perhaps I should try a different combination of parameters or see if there's another way to accomplish this.\n\nActually wait - let me try once more with a very minimal call, with just the required parameters `id` and `tag`, plus `high_limit` since that's what the user wants. The `low_limit` should default to null automatically. But we saw that even without specifying `low_limit`, the error occurs.\n\nThis seems to be a persistent bug. I should inform the user and potentially try to use `set_threshold` to update an existing alarm, or find another workaround.\n\nActually, let me re-read the function spec one more time:\n\n```json\n\"low_limit\": {\n    \"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"number\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}],\n    \"default\": null,\n    \"title\": \"Low Limit\"\n}\n```\n\nThe issue is clear - the default is `null` in JSON Schema but when it goes through the framework, it becomes the Python string \"None\" which fails validation.\n\nSince this is a tool limitation, I should explain to the user that there appears to be a technical issue with the alarm creation tool, specifically with the `low_limit` parameter handling.\n\nActually, I realize I should try different approaches. What if I explicitly set low_limit to a very small number like -Infinity or just a very negative number that's irrelevant? Let me try with a real number value.\n\nActually, I already tried `low_limit: -9999` and it still gave the same error about \"Input should be a valid number, unable to parse string as a number\". That's very suspicious - when I pass -9999 (a number), why would it say \"unable to parse string as a number\"?\n\nWait... let me look at this more carefully. The error message says \"low_limit: Input should be a valid number, unable to parse string as a number\". This suggests that even when I pass a number, it's somehow being converted to a string before validation.\n\nThis is clearly a framework bug. The `strict: false` flag doesn't seem to help here.\n\nLet me try one more thing - what if the issue is that all parameters are being passed as strings? Let me check if other numeric parameters have the same issue. Looking at `deadband` which is type number with default 0 - I passed `deadband: 0` and it didn't complain, but `low_limit` failed.\n\nActually wait - when I passed `deadband: 0` and `low_limit: -9999`, both should have been numbers, but only `low_limit` failed. Maybe there's something specific about how `low_limit` with the `anyOf` type is being handled differently.\n\nI think the issue is specifically with the `anyOf: [number, null]` type union. The framework might not handle this correctly.\n\nI should inform the user about this technical limitation and suggest alternatives.\n\nLet me think about alternatives:\n1. Maybe I can use `set_threshold` after creating the alarm (but the alarm doesn't exist yet and creation fails)\n2. Maybe I can use `set_limit` dynamic tool\n3. Maybe I can inform the user about the issue\n\nActually, let me try the dynamic `set_limit` tool - it might work differently.\n\nWait, I don't see a clear way to create the alarm without the tool working. Let me just try once more with the exact minimal required parameters only."}, {"turn": 10, "model": "mimo-v2.5-pro", "input_tokens": 49452, "output_tokens": 240, "latency_ms": 10177.59420000948, "stop_reason": "tool_use", "text": null, "reasoning": "好的，PT_COMP_01 点位确实存在，是一个模拟量点，单位是 MPa。\n\n现在创建报警的问题在于 `low_limit` 参数。让我试试用 Python 的 None 来表示 null。在参数列表中，我需要不传这个参数，让它使用默认值。\n\n让我再试一次，这次确保 JSON 格式正确。也许问题出在我之前的尝试中，`low_limit` 被设置为字符串 \"None\" 而不是 JSON null。\n\n根据函数定义，`low_limit` 的默认值是 `null`。如果我不传这个参数，应该会使用默认值。\n\n让我用最简单的参数格式再试一次："}, {"turn": 11, "model": "mimo-v2.5-pro", "input_tokens": 49740, "output_tokens": 595, "latency_ms": 21198.583399993367, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "text": "很抱歉，创建模拟量报警的工具目前存在一个技术问题——`low_limit` 参数的 schema 验证存在缺陷，即使传入合法的数值也会被误判为字符串而报错。\n\n**您需要的配置如下：**\n\n| 报警 ID | 监测点位 | 报警类型 | 报警限值 | 优先级 |\n|---------|----------|----------|----------|--------|\n| `alarm_PT_COMP_01_H` | PT_COMP_01 | 高限 (High) | 2.8 MPa | Medium |\n| `alarm_PT_COMP_01_HH` | PT_COMP_01 | 高高限 (High-High) | 3.2 MPa | High |\n\n**当前状态：**\n- ✅ 点位 `PT_COMP_01`（模拟量，单位 MPa）已存在\n- ❌ 报警创建失败（工具 `low_limit` 参数的框架级 schema 验证错误）\n\n**建议操作：**\n1. 联系系统管理员排查 `create_analog_alarm` 工具中 `low_limit` 参数的序列化问题\n2. 或者通过系统配置界面手动创建这两个报警\n\n需要我尝试其他方式来完成这个配置吗？", "reasoning": "This is very frustrating. The low_limit parameter is consistently failing regardless of what value I provide. This appears to be a systematic issue with how the tool processes the `low_limit` parameter - it seems to always be converting it to a string before validation, even when a number is explicitly provided.\n\nLet me think about this differently. Perhaps the issue is in how the framework serializes parameters. When I provide `low_limit=-1`, maybe the framework is serializing it as the string \"-1\" rather than the number -1.\n\nActually, looking at the error pattern, it seems like all `low_limit` values are being converted to strings before validation. This is a bug in the tool framework.\n\nSince this is a tool limitation that I can't work around, I should:\n1. Inform the user about the technical issue\n2. Try alternative approaches like using `set_limit` or checking if there's another way to create the alarm\n\nLet me try using the dynamic `set_limit` tool to see if it offers an alternative approach.\n\nActually, I just realized - the `set_limit` tool has no parameters defined in its schema (DynamicSetLimitArgs has no properties). This might be another dynamic stub.\n\nI think the best course of action is to:\n1. Acknowledge that the tool has a technical issue with the low_limit parameter\n2. Explain what I was trying to do\n3. Suggest that the user or system administrator may need to fix the tool or use an alternative method\n\nLet me inform the user clearly about what happened and what the intended configuration would be."}], "rag": {"enabled": false, "top_n": 30, "top_k": 12, "alpha": 0.6, "use_reranker": true}, "workflow": {"enabled": false, "selected_workflow": null}, "totals": {"input_tokens": 518785, "output_tokens": 4237, "cost_usd": 0.0, "e2e_latency_ms": 174415.83020001417}, "judge": null}
